Unreleased
---
Bugfix
- The popup surface said `dismissed` twice when Escape put it away - once for
  the key, once again for the going - and once when every other hand put it
  away. The answer is said by the going now, wherever the hand came from, and
  says itself once.
Feature
- `QxToolTip`, the note a control explains itself with: it watches its
  anchor, waits out an arrival before it opens, stays a while and goes, and
  is the page turned inside out - the ink as the surface, the page's colour
  as the text. It takes no mouse and no focus while it is up.
- `QxPopupSurface`, the surface a set of answers opens into: it is drawn and
  lifted off the page, it opens beside its anchor and keeps inside the
  screen, and it goes away when the keyboard asks it to or the pointer lands
  outside it. What a family carries on it - its radius and its content -
  stays with the family.
- `QxCalendarFlyout`, the month a date control opens: six weeks to the grid,
  the weekdays named over it, the arrows to walk the months, and the day
  taken standing in the accent - what falls outside the month dimmed rather
  than gone, today left as a ring so a click elsewhere is not a loss. The
  keyboard walks the days in all four directions, and the day is spoken once,
  when it is taken. Which font the head and the weekday row wear, and how
  deep the corners are drawn, is the family's to say.
- `QxTimeFlyout`, the two columns an hour and a minute are taken from: an
  hour of twenty-four beside a minute of sixty, six rows of each on show,
  the taken one standing in a pill of the accent. The wheel looks and does
  not take - a turn is not an answer - a click takes, and the arrow keys walk
  the column the keyboard sits in. The row's font is the family's to say.

0.1.0
---
Feature
- The library opens. It is the shared base the control libraries draw on:
  the mechanisms two design languages answer the same way - state layers,
  focus rings, shadows - kept apart from the forms, which stay with each
  family. It reads the token vocabularies from qxtheme and nothing above it.
- QxStateLayer, the wash a control draws over itself to say where the pointer
  is, where the focus sits, what is being pressed: default opacities per
  state, layered() as the value a test can predict, paint() as the draw.
  Which colour a family washes with belongs to the family; how a wash is
  composed and painted is here.
- QxShadowPainter, the mechanism that turns an elevation tier into pixels: a
  tile rendered once for a set of parameters - blur, alpha, corner radius,
  colour - and stretched over the rectangle in nine pieces, cached so the
  second card of the same shape costs a lookup. The blur is sixteen
  concentric rings rather than a true gaussian, which reads as one smooth
  falloff at shadow sizes and keeps the render deterministic and cheap.
- QxControl, the base a self-drawn control builds on. It tracks where the
  pointer is, what is being pressed and whether the keyboard is on it, and
  answers all three as one state; it draws the wash between the surface and
  the content, and the focus ring over both. A subclass says what its surface
  looks like, what its content is, and when the pointer counts as being on
  it, and inherits the rest. The paint order is why surface and content are
  two hooks and not one: a control that drew its glyphs in the surface hook
  would have the wash land on them.
- QxControl::chromeRect(), the part of a control that the wash and the focus
  ring belong to - the whole widget unless the control says otherwise. A
  control is often a small thing with a sentence beside it, and tinting the
  sentence is a hover that dims the label, not one that lights the control.
- QxControl::accessibleInterface(), the way a control with more to say than a
  role and a state supplies a face of its own and owns it. A slider's answer
  is a number, and an assistive tool wants to read that number and set it
  back - which neither a role nor a state can carry. A control that answers
  null, which is most of them, gets the face the base builds.
- QxControlAccessible, the accessible face of a control that paints itself,
  and the factory that hands one to every control of this base. A control
  drawn in code is invisible to an assistive tool in the way that matters:
  the pixels that say what it is and what is true of it are its own, so a
  control answers two questions - its role and its state - and the plumbing
  around the answers is done once, here. A control that does not say what it
  is gets the role a plain widget gets, which is the honest answer, not a
  guess.
- QxRingPainter, the focus ring drawn as a band rather than a stroke: the
  ring occupies the strip from its inset to its inset + width inside the
  rectangle, so it never falls off the control and never needs clipping, and
  a negative inset is how a ring hangs over the edge. A ring's corners turn
  with the control's radius, and painting one band - without seams at the
  corners - is what lives here; which bands a family draws is its own answer.
